<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  iOS Universal Links.

  This is the app's half of the claim; the site's half is served from
  app/.well-known/apple-app-site-association. Both are required, and the
  iOS failure mode is silent in the same way Android's is: links simply keep
  opening in Safari with no error anywhere.

  Capacitor does not generate this file, so it is hand-written and wired up
  through CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS in App.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj rather than
  through the Xcode navigator. It will not appear in the project file tree —
  that is expected and does not affect signing. Adding "Associated Domains" via
  Xcode's Signing & Capabilities tab writes this same pair of changes.

  Two further steps happen outside this repo and cannot be done from here:

    1. The Associated Domains capability must be enabled for the App ID in the
       Apple Developer portal, or signing fails with a provisioning error.
    2. APPLE_APP_TEAM_ID must be set in the deploy, or the association file
       404s and iOS finds nothing to verify against.

  Both surfaces are listed because they are separate hostnames served by the
  same Worker (see lib/panaverse/surfaces.ts) and this one app carries both.
  Verification is per-host: an unlisted host is an unclaimed host.

  iOS fetches these over Apple's CDN at install time. Edits to the served
  association file can take a while to propagate; during development, the
  "?mode=developer" behaviour and Settings > Developer > Associated Domains
  Development make iOS query the origin directly instead.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>applinks:panamia.club</string>
		<string>applinks:social.panamia.club</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
